<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Naiman Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1204 CE (approx.) · capital Altai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most westerly and literate of the Mongolian steppe powers before Genghis Khan, whose Uyghur scribes gave the Mongols their script.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Naimans were a large tribal people in western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> mountains and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a>, of Turkic or mixed Turkic and Mongolic origin; their name means 'the Eight' in Mongolian. By the 12th century they formed a khanate under a ruling family that bore Turkic titles.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Naimans were pastoral nomads in close contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-qocho">Kingdom of Qocho</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a>. Their court used Uyghur script and seals, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> had followers among them. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanch-bilge-khan">Inanch Bilge Khan</a> in the 1190s the khanate split between his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayang-khan">Tayang Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyruk-khan">Buyruk Khan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1204 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayang-khan">Tayang Khan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chakirmaut">Chakirmaut</a>, where Tayang died; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyruk-khan">Buyruk</a> was killed in 1206. Tayang's son Küchlüg fled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> and seized its throne. The captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> scribe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tata-tonga">Tata-tonga</a> taught the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> to write their language in Uyghur letters.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/naiman-khanate.jpg" alt="Naiman Khanate" width="320" height="209"><figcaption>Image: Khiruge · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naiman_Khanate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1204 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Altai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Inanch Bilge Khan, Tayang Khan, Buyruk Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism, Church of the East, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkic, Mongolic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Mongolia, China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Naimans</dd></dl></aside></div>

